- ODNCA-STD-003 "SNS-NAME-1 - Name Format Standard" —
https://odnca.org/docs/ODNCA-STD-003-SNS-NAME-1.pdf

Scheme syntax:
ordmail:<recipient>
recipient := <address> | <mailbox> "@" <name> "." <tld>
| <name> "." <tld> | <opns-name>
Colon-only form; producers MUST NOT emit "ordmail://". Names are UTF-8,
no whitespace or control characters. No DNS interaction at any point.

Scheme semantics: opens mail composition addressed to the recipient,
resolved through the client's name-resolution ladder (bare address,
mailbox at a name, SNS name, OpNS name — first match decides). Sending
the message is a separate, explicit user action; the URI itself never
sends anything.

Encoding considerations: UTF-8 names; percent-encoding per RFC 3986.
No IDNA/punycode processing.

Interoperability considerations: no DNS resolution and no relation to
"mailto:"; unaware applications treat it as an unknown scheme per
RFC 3986. No authority component, no default port.

Security considerations: composition only — handlers MUST NOT send
without an explicit user action. Recipient resolution follows the same
rules as the payment schemes: display the resolved destination, prefer
verification against committed on-chain state where available.
Visually confusable Unicode names carry the usual homograph risks.
